      "Major Drivers of Biodiversity Loss (The Evil Quartet): E.O. Wilson four primary anthropogenic drivers: Habitat destruction, loss, and fragmentation (clearing tropical forests for agriculture); Over-exploitation and illegal poaching (hunting elephants for ivory, tigers for skin and bones); Invasion of alien exotic species (Lantana camara, Parthenium hysterophorus, African catfish Clarias gariepinus outcompeting native fishes); Co-extinction (extinction of host plant leading to extinction of obligate insect pollinator).",
      "Threatened Species Categories (IUCN Red Data Book): International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List conservation status criteria: Extinct (EX), Extinct in the Wild (EW), Critically Endangered (CR: extreme risk of extinction, population crash over 80 percent), Endangered (EN: very high risk, e.g., Bengal tiger, Asiatic lion), Vulnerable (VU), Near Threatened (NT), and Least Concern (LC); Published in international Red Data Books.",
      "In Situ Conservation Frameworks (Protected Area Network): Preserving endangered wild species in their natural habitats: National Parks (strictly protected, no human exploitation or grazing permitted, e.g., Kaziranga, Corbett); Wildlife Sanctuaries (protection for specific fauna, limited grazing and human entry permitted, e.g., Periyar); Biosphere Reserves (UNESCO Man and the Biosphere MAB program: Core zone strictly protected, Buffer zone for non-destructive research, and Transition zone for sustainable human settlement; 18 Biosphere Reserves in India).",
      "Ex Situ Conservation Methodologies: Preserving endangered species outside their native habitats in artificial human-managed facilities: Botanical Gardens (cultivating threatened flora), Zoological Parks and Safari Reserves (captive breeding of endangered mammals and birds), Gene Banks, Seed Banks (dehydrated seeds stored at minus 20 degrees Celsius), Cryopreservation (freezing tissues, gametes, and embryos in liquid nitrogen at minus 196 degrees Celsius), and Tissue culture germplasm repositories.",
      "Flagship Species Conservation Programs in India: Targeted national ecological initiatives: Project Tiger (launched in 1973, establishing dedicated Tiger Reserves to protect apex predator and intact forest landscapes); Project Elephant (1992, securing elephant corridors and mitigating human-elephant conflict); Project Rhino (one-horned rhinoceros conservation in Kaziranga); Sacred groves (traditional community-protected virgin forest patches conserved through religious traditions)."
